                    Sealcoating doesn't repair asphalt—it prevents the damage that makes repairs necessary. Think of it like sunscreen for your driveway: a thin sacrificial layer that takes the abuse so the pavement underneath doesn't have to.
                </p>

                <p>
                    "Sealcoating" and "sealing" are often used interchangeably, and for the homeowner they amount to the same thing: applying a protective liquid coating over an existing <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asphalt_concrete" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">asphalt</a> surface. The term "sealcoating" is borrowed from the commercial paving world, where crews seal everything from driveways to sprawling parking lots. Whatever you call it, the purpose is identical—to shield the asphalt binder from the forces that age and destroy it.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Here's why it matters so much. Asphalt is held together by a petroleum-based binder that's constantly being broken down by UV radiation, water, and chemical spills. As the binder degrades, the pavement turns gray, brittle, and porous, then cracks and crumbles. A sealcoat replenishes that surface, blocks UV and water, fills surface voids, and restores the deep black finish. In this guide we'll cover exactly what sealcoating is, how often to do it, what it costs DIY versus professionally, the best products to use, and a complete step-by-step application process.
                </p>

                <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-6 font-sans">
                    What Sealcoating Actually Does (And Doesn't Do)
                </h2>

                <p>
                    A huge amount of homeowner confusion comes from misunderstanding what a sealcoat can and can't accomplish. Setting expectations correctly is the key to being satisfied with the result.
                </p>

                <p><strong>What sealcoating does:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Blocks UV degradation.</strong> The coating absorbs the sun's rays so the asphalt binder beneath stays flexible instead of oxidizing into brittle gray.</li>
                    <li><strong>Waterproofs the surface.</strong> It seals the porous top layer so water can't penetrate to the base, stopping the freeze-thaw cycle that creates potholes.</li>
                    <li><strong>Resists chemicals.</strong> It forms a barrier against the oil, gas, and brake fluid that would otherwise dissolve the petroleum-based pavement.</li>
                    <li><strong>Restores appearance.</strong> It returns that rich, uniform black color, dramatically boosting curb appeal.</li>
                    <li><strong>Fills minor surface voids.</strong> Sand-loaded sealcoats fill micro-cracks and restore traction to worn surfaces.</li>
                </ul>

                <p><strong>What sealcoating does NOT do:</strong> It is not a structural repair. It cannot fix potholes, fill cracks wider than ⅛ inch, level depressions, or rescue a failed base. All of that must be handled <em>before</em> sealcoating using dedicated <a href="/asphalt-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">asphalt repair</a> and <a href="/best-asphalt-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">crack filling</a> products. Sealcoat is the final protective step, not a cure for existing damage.
                </p>

