                <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans">
                    How Often Should You Sealcoat?
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Timing is where most people go wrong in both directions—some never seal, others seal far too often. The sweet spot for a residential asphalt driveway is <strong>every 2 to 3 years</strong>. This schedule keeps a strong protective barrier in place while letting the pavement breathe between coats.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Sealing every single year is a genuine mistake. Sealcoat builds up into an increasingly thick slab that develops surface tension as it dries, leading to hairline cracking and flaking. More is not better. On the other hand, going five-plus years between coats lets the binder oxidize and the surface become porous, at which point cracks form and you've lost the protective window.
                </p>
                <p>
                    A quick field test: pour a cup of water on the driveway. If it beads up and sits on the surface, your sealcoat is still working. If it soaks straight in and darkens the asphalt, the surface is porous and it's time to reseal. Also watch the color—when the deep black has faded to gray, the UV protection is spent.
                </p>

                <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-6 my-10 rounded-r-2xl">
                    <p class="m-0 text-gray-800"><strong>Important timing note:</strong> Never sealcoat a brand-new asphalt driveway. Fresh asphalt needs to cure and release its volatile oils for a full <strong>6 to 12 months</strong> before its first sealcoat. Sealing too early traps those oils and leaves a permanently soft, tacky surface.</p>
                </div>

                <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans">
                    Sealcoating Cost: DIY vs Professional
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Sealcoating is one of the highest-ROI maintenance jobs precisely because the materials are cheap and the labor is straightforward. Let's break down the real numbers for a typical 600-square-foot residential driveway.
                </p>

                <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 my-8 shadow-md">
